# Linux-C.O.S -- defensive / blue-team layer, tier 1: plain Debian/Kali apt
# packages (no extra repo needed). Kali's own kali-tools-detect / -protect /
# -respond / -recover / -identify categories already cover a slice of this
# (see kali-security.list.chroot) -- this file is the rest of the classic
# blue-team stack that Kali doesn't ship as a menu category.
suricata
suricata-update
yara
clamav
clamav-daemon
clamav-freshclam
aide
aide-common
lynis
rkhunter
tiger
wireshark
tshark
tcpdump
osquery
wazuh-agent

# NOT in this list on purpose -- falco and sysdig both ship a DKMS/eBPF
# kernel driver that their postinst tries to *compile* right there in the
# package install step, against whatever kernel is running. Inside a
# live-build chroot that's the build container's own kernel (Docker
# Desktop's linuxkit VM on this Mac) -- not the kernel the finished image
# will actually boot with -- so the compile fails and dpkg aborts the whole
# build. This is a real "the driver must be built where it will run"
# problem, not a naming/pinning mistake: install these two AFTER boot on
# the target machine instead (`apt-get install falco sysdig` -- both repos
# are already configured on the image), where DKMS can build against the
# real running kernel.

# Not in this list either -- Debian doesn't package these under these names
# at all (zeek, ntopng, snort each need their own upstream apt repo, the
# same way falco/osquery/wazuh do above; nobody's wired those repos up yet,
# see build/README.md TODO). Don't just apt-get these in a hook without
# adding a config/archives/*.list.chroot for them first -- see git history
# on this file for exactly that mistake and how it was fixed.
